{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And that which shall be redeemed from among the Levites.</b> It appears from the plain sense of this verse that when He said, \"a perpetual redemption it shall be for the Levites\" (Leviticus 25:32), He meant to redeem from the hand [of the buyer] and to redeem permanently, but He did not specify among them whether the relatives redeem or whether the Jubilee applies to them. Therefore He went back and said, \"And that which shall be redeemed from among the Levites\" — the redeemer who redeems shall be either the man himself or the Levite who is his relative — \"and it shall go out from the sale of the house and the city of his possession in the Jubilee,\" from the hand of the buyer, on this condition does he redeem, that he shall reckon the years of his sale and return the surplus — to say that the Jubilee applies to them, and the redemption of the Levites shall be as He said concerning Israel: \"and he shall reckon the years of his sale\" etc. (Leviticus 25:27). And our Sages said (Arakhin 33a) that the verse speaks of a Levite who purchased from a Levite. And according to their words, it appears to me in the interpretation of the verse that formerly in Israel, when a person needed to sell his field, the one who came first in his inheritance would come and acquire it, and this is what is called \"redemption,\" as it is said: \"Buy the field that is in Anathoth, for yours is the right of redemption to buy it\" (Jeremiah 32:7). And so it is further explicit in the matter of Boaz (Ruth 4). And it appears to me that they had a law of priority, as our Sages established for us in the law of bar metzra, and they would acquire from the first redeemer by means of a kerchief-act of acquisition, just as Boaz did (ibid. 4:8). Now the verse says here that when the Levite redeems the inheritance of his Levite relative — that is, when he acquires it from him according to the law of redemption — that sale shall go out in the Jubilee. This teaches that the Jubilee applies to them in redemption just as in any other sale. And there was no need to mention the law regarding sale alone, for they are included among Israel, except that He gave them additional strength through the perpetual redemption. And this law of redemption applies also to Israel, but the verse mentioned it regarding the Levites lest you say: the cities were given only to the tribe of Levi as a whole, and therefore they would revert from [the possession of] Israel but not from [another] Levite, and all the more so from a close relative whom we have obligated to acquire — therefore He said that even [an acquisition through] redemption goes out in the Jubilee, and from the Levite we learn [the law] also for Israel. And it is also possible that the Levites are not permitted to sell their cities to Israel under any manner of sale, and therefore every acquisition of theirs is called \"redemption.\""], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:00:41.072796050Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}